Subject: [PATCH] btrfs: exact-generation quiescent subvolume destruction
Base-commit: cb444174e58f61ebed6c4140af0579cdcc6fa1e1
Base-tag: 6.18.57-xanmod1
Source: https://gitlab.com/xanmod/linux/-/tree/cb444174e58f61ebed6c4140af0579cdcc6fa1e1

Lineage: completed preserved 6.18.56-xanmod1 scratch first; rebased onto
the owner-approved full 6.18.57-xanmod1 stable update, not selected backports.
The original scratch and completed original-base patch are preserved.

Add the downstream 96-byte BTRFS_IOC_SNAP_DESTROY_QUIESCENT ABI, request
0x406094ff on x86_64. Match FSID, subvolume ID, native UUID and received
UUID, reject flags/reserved input, and require a readonly subvolume.
This is not an upstream ioctl allocation or an ordinary-delete fallback.

Count every production inode/root association until irreversible inode
teardown, including I_NEW, unhashed synthetic directories and replaced or
dying objects absent from the inode xarray. Dummy metadata inodes and new
subvolume inodes are counted too; sanity-test inodes have their separate
test destructor and never enter this production accounting. Count ownership
includes a root reference. The xarray is only a cache-eviction aid.

Close cached iget and new publication with a per-root rwsem. Admissions and
closure use trylocks: do not wait behind deletion while holding transaction
or inode locks. Internal relocation/cache walkers may take temporary inode
references but cannot publish files, dentries or mounts. Canonical READY
publication is required before a filesystem root can instantiate inodes.

Register every root before reading its disk item. An immutable constructor
ID avoids reading root_key while it is initialized. Registry removal happens
only at final root put, under the same spinlock used to grab nonzero refs;
zero references cannot revive and imply zero counted inodes. Linked deletion
rejects preexisting same-ID constructors and publishes closure atomically.
New constructors inherit closure; duplicates retry the canonical object,
never publish a stale copy. READY is sampled under the registry lock before
reading root-item identity. subvol_sem serializes identity/readonly changes.

For linked deletion, hold parent/victim inode locks, then subvol_sem, then
the nonblocking publication gate. Unhash (do not invalidate a foreign mount),
drain pre-unhash RCU walks and prune unused clean dentries. Require exactly
the administrative root inode, and inspect inode plus dentry counts together
under i_lock then d_lock. Failure rehashes the same dentry and reopens only
an intact pre-unlink root. Successful unlink leaves that incarnation closed.

ENOENT requires a separate absent-generation proof, not pathname or radix
absence. Pin any canonical root across the entire census; otherwise inspect
the live root tree. A transient per-ID constructor barrier spans disk lookup
and the complete orphan registry scan, including final-free gaps with no
surviving root. All matching ready orphan objects must have exact identity,
be readonly, have publication closed and have zero live inodes. Incomplete
constructors return EBUSY without reading mutable identity. Clear the barrier
atomically with the final registry scan; surviving objects remain closed.
Subsequent external handle/mount lookup rejects missing or zero-ref roots.

The registry spinlock is never held across sleeping work or root puts.
Cursor and canonical references stabilize objects across cache pruning.
No global VFS gate, new syscall dispatcher or process-owned lease is added.
Normal VFS ownership covers mounts/private OverlayFS lowers, O_PATH, files,
mappings, cwd/root/exe, SCM_RIGHTS, registered io_uring and loop backing files.
Dirty cache is not discarded to manufacture quiescence. Busy or temporary
constructor/cache activity may conservatively prevent deletion.

Preserve the first unlink error through transaction teardown. Any error once
unlink starts becomes EIO, never ENOENT or a safe pre-unlink rejection.
Filesystem-error state also returns EIO; recovery must reconcile exact disk
identity and transaction state. No old references survive a kernel reboot.

Source implementation only: no compilation, tests, formatter, VM execution,
installation, guard activation or reboot is represented by this artifact.
Parent-owned review/build and isolated actual-operation safety smoke remain
necessary before installing or booting a kernel on authoritative data.

